# Neutral Commit Language

## The Problem

Commit messages that say "remove prohibited terms" or "sanitize internal references" tell anyone reading the history that something was being hidden. The commit message itself becomes the disclosure. This is a public repository — the git log is permanent.

## Commit Format

All commits MUST follow Conventional Commits:

```
<type>(<scope>): <description>
```

Valid types: `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `docs`, `test`, `chore`, `perf`

Valid scopes: `extension`, `webview`, `test`, `ci`, `schema`, `syntax`

## Prohibited Patterns in Commit Messages

NEVER write a commit message that contains any of the following:

| Prohibited Pattern | Why |
|---|---|
| "remove prohibited ..." | Announces that a prohibition list exists |
| "sanitize internal ..." | Announces that internal terminology was present |
| "remove internal references" | Same |
| "drop internal ..." | Same |
| "scrub ..." (in a cleanup context) | Implies something was being concealed |
| "clean up proprietary ..." | Announces proprietary content existed |
| "enforce alignment with ..." | Implies a hidden constraint |
| "Phase 1", "Phase 2", "Phase N" | Exposes internal development milestones |

## What to Write Instead

Describe the change in terms of what improved — not what was removed or corrected.

| Wrong | Right |
|---|---|
| `remove prohibited terms and fix broken links` | `fix broken links and update terminology` |
| `sanitize internal terminology` | `standardize terminology` |
| `remove internal references from source code` | `update terminology in source code` |
| `drop internal field-group numbering` | `simplify field-group headings` |
| `clean up proprietary tool references` | `update tool references in documentation` |

## The Underlying Principle

Write every commit message as if this is the only version of reality. There was no "before" state with internal terms — there is only the current, clean state. The commit improves, standardizes, simplifies, or updates. It never removes, sanitizes, scrubs, or cleans up something that "should not have been there."

User instructions are private. Commit messages are public. Keep them separate.
